In this tutorial, we are going to learn about the use of an "if" statement in C#.

The if statement is used to execute a certain block of code if a condition is true.

## Syntax

Let us look at the syntax of an "if" statement in C#.

```csharp
if (condition) {
// code
}
```

The condition is an expression which must return a boolean value (true or false).

If the condition is true, then the code inside the block is executed, otherwise, it is skipped.

## Example

Let's write an "if" statement in C# which checks if a person is old enough to vote.

The voting age in most countries is 18. So, we will check if the person is 18 or older.

```csharp
using System;

namespace HelloWord {
class Program {
static void Main(string[] args) {
int age = 19;

if (age >= 18) {
Console.WriteLine("The person is old enough to vote.");
}
}
}
}
```

Let's compile and run the program.

Output

```
The person is old enough to vote.
```

We first define the variable age and set its value to 19. Then we check if the age is greater than or equal to 18. Since the age is 19, the condition is true and the code inside the block is executed.

If you change the value of age to a number less than 18, then the condition would be false and the code inside the block is not executed.

## else statement

We can also use an "else" statement along with the "if" statement.

The code inside the "else" block is executed when the condition is false.

```csharp
if (condition) {
// code if condition is true
} else {
// code if condition is false
}
```

